  "articleBody": "It's easy for small spaces to feel overwhelming if they're not designed with intention! We often see homeowners make common mistakes that lead to clutter and inefficiency. First off, a big one is underestimating the need for multi-functional furniture. If your coffee table can't also store blankets or expand for dining, you're missing out on precious real estate. Think about pieces that serve dual purposes to keep things tidy and adaptable. Another frequent issue is a lack of vertical storage. Walls are gold in tiny homes! Instead of letting items sprawl horizontally, look up. Shelving, wall-mounted organizers, and even hooks can transform unused wall space into functional storage, freeing up your floor. Many folks also don't declutter ruthlessly enough before moving into or designing a tiny space; everything needs a purpose. You really have to get honest with yourself about what you truly need and use daily. Lastly, overlooking hidden storage opportunities like under-bed drawers, toe-kick drawers in cabinets, or built-in benches can make a huge difference. Every nook and cranny has potential. By thinking smart about every inch, you can transform a cluttered feeling into one of spacious calm. If you're still struggling, chatting with a small-space specialist can open up new possibilities you hadn't considered.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-02"
